paragraph('The questionnaire first collects',r'''The initial questionnaire asks about teachers' demographic and professional circumstances: their age and gender, years of teaching, educational stage, employment situation, school ownership and number of teaching groups. Teachers then consider hypothetical students and choose whether each should progress or repeat. The cards describe gender, social background, failed subjects, basic competences, attendance and discipline. Two examples introduce this task before the eight experimental cards.

The final questionnaire asks teachers to assess their influence on students, describe their empathy, and judge the role of effort rather than luck or family circumstances in economic outcomes. It also asks about resources for students who repeat, including agreement that too many resources are devoted to them. These questions capture professional self-perceptions and broader beliefs alongside views directly relevant to repetition. Figure~\ref{fig:timeline} shows when the two questionnaires and the decisions occur.''')
t=t.replace(' Shared-study assignments are described briefly in Appendix~\\ref{app:sample}.','')
attention=re.search(r'\\old\{Before the task,.*?\}',baseline,re.S).group(0)
paragraph('Figure~\\ref{fig:timeline} distinguishes',attention)
start=t.index(r'\subsection{Teacher information and analysis samples}')
end=t.index(r'\section{Decision framework and empirical strategy}',start)
t=t[:start]+t[end:]
paragraph('The additive model includes',r'''The additive model includes all six attributes. An interaction model adds failed subjects interacted with competences, absence and discipline, with Holm adjustment across those three interaction tests. The within-teacher analysis asks which information changes recommendations, while the teacher-level models examine differences in harshness. Student attributes are denoted by $x_i$; $X_1$--$X_3$ below label sets of teacher covariates.''')
paragraph('We present the distribution of',r'''We present the distribution of $H^b$ before examining whether a teacher's relative recommendations recur across different subsets of answers. We repeat 1,000 random splits among teachers with at least eight responses, using reference samples external to the evaluated teachers and disjoint between the two halves. Appendix~\ref{app:harshness} explains the procedure. We then use terciles to describe how attribute coefficients differ across the harshness distribution.''')
paragraph('Harshness is the dependent variable',r'''Harshness is the dependent variable in the teacher-level models. The professional and demographic set, $X_1$, contains educational stage, experience, gender, age, school ownership, reported permanent public appointment and number of teaching groups. $X_2$ adds perceived teacher impact, empathy and attribution of economic position to effort rather than luck. $X_3$ adds agreement that too many resources are allocated to repeaters, on its original 0--10 scale. The effort item runs from 1 (luck, family circumstances and contacts) to 10 (effort, education and merit). Age and experience enter jointly; school tenure is omitted. This separates differences associated with age from those associated with time spent teaching.

All three specifications use the same 2,157 complete cases. OLS associations use HC3 standard errors, supplemented by 999 teacher bootstrap draws that reconstruct the card references. Prediction uses five repetitions of five teacher folds. All methods share the same targets and partitions. A held-out teacher contributes neither to training nor to the card reference against which that teacher's answers are evaluated.''')
# Preserve the substantive academic contrast, simplify numerical narration.
paragraph('The within-teacher coefficients',r'''The within-teacher coefficients in Table~\ref{tab:attr} show a clear hierarchy of information. Three or more failed subjects increase the recommendation probability by about 55 percentage points, and low basic competences by about 27 points. The control-only estimates have similar magnitudes. These academic indicators convey different information: failed subjects refer to formal performance, whereas competences describe preparedness more broadly.''')
paragraph('The male-student coefficient',r'''Student gender makes little difference to the average recommendation: the male-student coefficient is only 0.26 percentage points and is not statistically distinguishable from zero. A complex social background reduces repetition recommendations by 1.88 points. These results contrast with the higher population incidence of repetition among boys and disadvantaged students \cite{oecd}. They are compatible with some disparities arising from the academic and school circumstances students bring to the promotion decision, rather than an additional penalty attached directly to their group. The background result is also compatible with teachers taking difficult circumstances into account in a compensatory way. This comparison concerns judgments on otherwise comparable profiles, not the full educational process through which population gaps arise.''')
paragraph('The interaction specification',r'''The interaction specification in Table~\ref{tab:interactions} shows that failed subjects and low competences reinforce one another. Low competences add about 22 percentage points when the failed-subject indicator is absent and 32 points when it is present; the interaction remains significant after adjustment for the three interaction tests. Interactions of failures with attendance or discipline are smaller and imprecise. Thus teachers respond especially strongly when both academic signals indicate difficulty. The next section examines what happens when those signals point in different directions.''')
paragraph('Harshness exhibits moderate consistency',r'''Harshness exhibits moderate consistency within the task: teachers who recommend repetition more often than their peers in one half of their answers tend to do so in the other half as well. The mean correlation across 1,000 random splits is 0.483. Figure~\ref{fig:halves} in Appendix~\ref{app:harshness} shows the variation across splits and explains the independent reference construction. This provides a check that the differences visible in the distribution are not confined to one particular subset of responses.''')
paragraph('The attribute coefficients are not homogeneous',r'''The attribute coefficients differ across these descriptive groups. The failed-subject coefficient is about 33 points in the lower tercile and 65 points in the other two, whereas the competence coefficient stays between roughly 25 and 29 points. Absence and discipline have clearer positive coefficients in the lower tercile. In the upper tercile, absence is close to zero and discipline has a negative coefficient. Because terciles are formed from the same responses, these regressions describe the observed patterns rather than identifying three distinct teacher types. In particular, the negative discipline coefficient can reflect the selection of responses into the upper tercile. The continuous analysis below asks whether observed teacher information helps explain the overall differences.''')
paragraph('Table~\\ref{tab:char} estimates',r'''Table~\ref{tab:char} compares models of harshness on 2,157 teachers. The full specification accounts for less than five percent of its observed variation. Secondary teachers are somewhat more severe on average, and older teachers less severe when compared at the same level of experience. Experience itself has a small, uncertain coefficient. These differences coexist with substantial overlap between the groups: knowing a teacher's career profile provides only limited information about their judgments.''')
paragraph('Agreement that too many resources',r'''Agreement that too many resources are devoted to repeaters has the clearest belief association. A five-point difference on the 0--10 scale corresponds to about 4.4 percentage points of harshness. The positive association with effort attribution becomes less precise once resource agreement is included; perceived impact and empathy add little. Appendix~\ref{app:harshness} shows that rebuilding the card references in teacher bootstrap samples supports the same broad interpretation.''')
paragraph('These associations yield only modest prediction',r'''These associations yield only modest prediction for new teachers (Table~\ref{tab:prediction}). Full OLS achieves a test $R^2$ of 3.72 percent, compared with 2.02 percent using professional and demographic variables alone. The practical improvement is small: the typical prediction error falls by only about a third of a percentage point. Ridge performs almost identically to full OLS, and the tuned forest does not improve on it. The paired comparisons support this reading; prediction from basic covariates in the larger available sample also remains weak (Appendix~\ref{app:prediction}).''')
paragraph('The decision-level decomposition',r'''The decision-level decomposition in Figure~\ref{fig:variance} asks how this limited teacher information compares with what is known about the student. Student attributes account for almost half of the model-implied variation in recommendations. Professional and demographic characteristics and reported beliefs together account for less than one percent. A further eight percent is associated with differences between teachers that these measured characteristics do not capture, while about 43 percent remains at the decision level. The decomposition therefore distinguishes knowing the student's circumstances from knowing the teacher's observed profile. The figure reports the full allocation and its bootstrap standard errors; Appendix~\ref{app:prediction} defines the model and denominator.''')
t=t.replace('95 percent intervals from 399 teacher bootstrap draws','one bootstrap standard error on either side of each estimate, based on 399 teacher resamples')
paragraph('The residual teacher share remains',r'''The residual teacher component remains when we restrict the analysis to teachers with at least eight responses or simplify the attribute specification. Its magnitude depends on how the binary decision is modeled: the logistic sensitivity assigns about twelve percent to it, compared with eight percent in the linear model. Both specifications therefore support the same substantive conclusion. Limited prediction from familiar teacher descriptors coexists with differences between teachers that those descriptors do not capture.''')
